<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Løgting</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Faroese Parliament</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Løgting is the parliament of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faroe-islands">Faroe Islands</a>. It descends from the Alting, the assembly of free men that met at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tinganes">Tinganes</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torshavn">Tórshavn</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viking-age">Viking Age</a> and is counted among the oldest parliamentary bodies in the world. Under Norwegian and then Danish rule it became mainly a law court and was abolished in 1816, but it was restored in 1852 and gained legislative powers under the Home Rule Act of 1948.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/logting.jpg" alt="Løgting" width="320" height="375" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Marmelad · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coat_of_arms_of_the_Faroe_Islands.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The name means Law Thing, a law assembly, from the Old Norse lǫgþing. A ting has existed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faroe-islands">Faroes</a> for over a millennium, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> era the Løgting was the highest authority on the islands, an alþing with both legislative and judicial authority and no executive power. The earliest sources place it on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tinganes">Tinganes</a>, now the oldest neighbourhood of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torshavn">Tórshavn</a>; Vikings liked to hold a ting in a neutral, uninhabited place. Its founding is not documented, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faereyinga-saga">Færeyinga saga</a> mentions it as well established in an account of a mid-10th century dispute between the chieftains Havgrímur and Einar Suðuroyingur. It was an assembly of the best men, wealthier farmers in a kind of republic of about 4,000 people.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1274 King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnus-vi-of-norway">Magnus VI of Norway</a> introduced the Norwegian Landslog, and the ting became an assembly of representatives of six local vártings with only judicial authority, its president, the Løgmaður, appointed by the king. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faroe-islands">Faroes</a> gained their first constitution on 24 June 1298, the Seyðabrævið, or Sheep Letter. Around 1380 they came with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> under the Danish throne. The Løgting kept some influence until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/absolute-monarchy">absolute monarchy</a> was introduced under Frederick III in 1660, and it was abolished in 1816, its judicial work passing to other courts.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>'s free constitution of 1849 ended the islands' special status without consulting the Faroese, many of whom wished to see the Løgting reinstated. The Act of 23 March 1852 reconstituted it as an advisory body, which first met on Ólavsøka in 1852. It became the platform of the Faroese nationalist movement, whose chief demand was home rule with legislative powers. Its protocols have been in Faroese since 1927.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> the United Kingdom occupied the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faroe-islands">Faroes</a> on 12 April 1940, and the Løgting functioned as a legislative assembly. At a vote on 14 September 1946 the electorate chose between a Danish proposal of home rule and secession, and 161 more votes favoured secession. The Danish government contested the legality of the process, and the king dissolved the parliament. A later election gave parties favouring union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> a majority of 2,000 votes, and the Home Rule Act came into force on 1 April 1948.</p><p>Both sides still argue over the 1946 result: some hold that a majority was a majority, others that so small a margin was too little for so weighty a decision. Under home rule the Løgting legislates in the matters taken over from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, while defence and foreign policy lie outside it.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Løgting” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The islanders governed themselves through the <b>Alting</b> at Tinganes, in what is now Tórshavn, one of the oldest parliamentary sites in the world.</blockquote><a href="/state/faroese-commonwealth">Faroese Commonwealth</a></li></ul></section>
